  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=-\\frac{3}{2{s}^{4}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}}=\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying the inverse Laplace transform to the above result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{{t}^{3}}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}} \\\\ {t}^{3} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}} & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplicative property of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}}={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{3}{2{s}^{4}}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{-\\frac{3}{2{s}^{4}}}=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
